{% from "_macros.html.j2" import means, does_not_show, ref with context %}
{% set c = q2.CZE %}
{% set fall = c.fall %}
<p class="close-kicker">Question 2 · Czech hockey atlas · 1995/96–{{ q1.season }}</p>
<h1 class="slide-q">{{ cur.title }}</h1>
<p class="slide-a">The observed Czech NHL count rose to {{ q1.peak.n }} players in {{ q1.peak.season }} and was {{ q1.peak.latest.n }} in {{ q1.peak.latest.season }}{% if fall %}; the break model places a downward step most likely in <strong>{{ fall.modal.season }}</strong>, with a posterior probability of only {{ f2(fall.modal.prob) }} for that season, a factor of {{ times(fall.delta_factor.median) }} (90 % HDI {{ f2(fall.delta_factor.lo) }}–{{ f2(fall.delta_factor.hi) }}) and P(level falls) = {{ f2(fall.p_down) }}{% if c.fall_spread %}, its probability spread over {{ c.fall_spread[0] }}–{{ c.fall_spread[1] }}{% endif %}{% endif %}.</p>
<figure class="figure-bleed" data-chart="q2-break">
  <table class="fig-table">
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Nation</th><th scope="col">Step</th><th scope="col">Most likely season (posterior probability)</th><th scope="col" class="num">Level after ÷ before, median (90 % HDI)</th><th scope="col" class="num">P(level rises)</th><th scope="col">Reading</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for code in ['CZE'] + q2.compared %}{% for s in q2[code].steps %}
      <tr{% if code == 'CZE' %} class="is-home"{% endif %}><td>{{ q2[code].name }}</td><td class="num">{{ s.order }}</td><td>{{ s.modal.season }} ({{ f2(s.modal.prob) }})</td><td class="num">{{ times(s.delta_factor.median) }} ({{ f2(s.delta_factor.lo) }}–{{ f2(s.delta_factor.hi) }})</td><td class="num">{{ f2(s.p_up) }}</td><td>{{ s.reading }}</td></tr>
    {% endfor %}{% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <figcaption><strong>Figure 1.</strong> Czech players with at least the games threshold in the NHL per season (dots; administrative counts, {{ c.n_obs }} observed seasons of {{ c.n_seasons }}), the model level (line) and its 90 % HDI (band), and the posterior probability that a step occurs in each season (bars). How to read it: ink bars are the step whose median factor is below 1, pale bars the step whose median is above 1; a tall single bar would be a sharp change. The line and band are drawn at the most probable pair of step seasons ({{ c.top_pair.seasons|join(' and ') }}), whose posterior probability is {{ f2(c.top_pair.prob) }}; they are conditional on that pair, and so is the model level of {{ c.fitted.latest.level|round|int }} in {{ c.fitted.latest.season }} against {{ c.fitted.peak.level|round|int }} in {{ c.fitted.peak.season }} ({{ pct0(c.fitted.latest_to_peak) }}).{% if fall and c.fall_spread %} For Czechia, {{ pct0(c.fall_mass_in_spread) }} of the downward step's probability lies over {{ c.fall_spread[0] }}–{{ c.fall_spread[1] }}, with no season above {{ f2(fall.modal.prob) }}.{% endif %} The table reads a step as a rise or a fall only when its 90 % HDI excludes 1.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section class="q-evidence" aria-labelledby="q2-evidence">
  <h2 class="q-sub" id="q2-evidence">Evidence</h2>
  <p class="slide-did"><span class="slide-did-label">The model</span> A Poisson local-level model, the same model the <a href="https://football.bsandova.com/">football atlas</a> uses for its series: the log of the expected count follows a random walk plus exactly {{ c.n_breaks }} step changes, and the step seasons are marginalised over every allowed pair (at least three seasons from the ends and from each other). Two steps are imposed on every nation, so a nation always receives two steps whether or not its series has them; the direction of a step is read from its interval, not assumed. 2004/05, the lockout, stays in the time index with no likelihood. Priors: σ ~ {{ q2.definitions.priors.sigma }}, step ~ {{ q2.definitions.priors.delta }}, initial level ~ {{ q2.definitions.priors.mu0 }}; step seasons {{ q2.definitions.priors.breaks }}.</p>
  {% if fall %}<p class="slide-did"><span class="slide-did-label">The Czech steps</span> The downward step is {{ times(fall.delta_factor.median) }} (90 % HDI {{ f2(fall.delta_factor.lo) }}–{{ f2(fall.delta_factor.hi) }}); the probability that it lowers the level is {{ f2(fall.p_down) }}, and its interval includes 1. The earlier step is dated most likely to {{ c.ups[0].modal.season if c.ups else '–' }}{% if c.ups %} ({{ times(c.ups[0].delta_factor.median) }}, 90 % HDI {{ f2(c.ups[0].delta_factor.lo) }}–{{ f2(c.ups[0].delta_factor.hi) }}, P(level rises) = {{ f2(c.ups[0].p_up) }}){% endif %}. Of the {{ q2.n_steps }} steps in the table, {{ q2.n_resolved }} {{ 'has an interval' if q2.n_resolved == 1 else 'have intervals' }} that exclude{{ 's' if q2.n_resolved == 1 else '' }} 1.</p>{% endif %}
  <p class="slide-how"><span class="slide-how-label">Diagnostics</span> {{ q2.definitions.sampler.chains }} chains × {{ q2.definitions.sampler.draws }} draws after {{ q2.definitions.sampler.tune }} tuning, seed {{ q2.definitions.sampler.seed }}; largest R-hat {{ '%.3f'|format(q2.max_rhat) }}, {{ q2.divergences }} divergent transition{{ '' if q2.divergences == 1 else 's' }} over the three fits. HDI: highest-density interval of the posterior.</p>
</section>
{% call means() %}
<p>The observed Czech NHL count fell from {{ q1.peak.n }} in {{ q1.peak.season }} to {{ q1.peak.latest.n }} in {{ q1.peak.latest.season }}, and the model does not place that fall in one season: the most likely season for a downward step carries {{ pct0(fall.modal.prob) if fall else '–' }} of the probability, and the step's interval includes no change. The data are consistent with a gradual decline. The model describes when the level changed; it does not say why, and the timing coincides with other changes in the leagues that it cannot separate.</p>
{% endcall %}
{% call does_not_show() %}
<li>Cause. The upward steps in all three nations around 1998/99–2000/01 coincide with the NHL's growth from 26 to 30 teams {{ ref(['nhl_timeline'], prefix ~ 'methodology/') }}, and the Czech decline overlaps the founding of the KHL in 2008 {{ ref(['khl'], prefix ~ 'methodology/') }}, a league the atlas does not cover. Neither is tested here.</li>
<li>A forecast of the count.</li>
{% endcall %}
